meta:
  version: '3.0'
  architecture: cluster-before-extract
  total_examples: 27
  convergent_positives: 18
  hard_negatives: 9
  expected_coverage:
  - convergent_multi_source_synthesis
  - single_source_rejection
  - platitude_detection
  - mechanism_boundary_consequence_structure
  - one_to_n_extraction
  - false_convergence_detection
  - citation_echo_detection
  - all_property_coverage
  calibration_status: needs_review
  calibrated_date: null
  notes: 'D2205: P0 fix pass on D2204 set. NEG-001..004 restructured to route: NULL

    (NEG-CONV pattern). CONV-003 fabricated sources replaced (Forceville 1996 +

    McQuarrie & Mick 1999). NEG-005 jargon echo + NEG-006 boundary violation added.

    9 hard negatives across 6 categories: single-source (NEG-CONV-001, NEG-001),

    platitude (NEG-CONV-002, NEG-002), false convergence (NEG-CONV-003, NEG-003),

    citation echo (NEG-004), jargon echo (NEG-005), boundary violation (NEG-006).

    Run LLM eval prompt before calibration.'
examples:
- id: CONV-001
  domain: pricing
  source_books:
  - "Predictably Irrational \u2014 Dan Ariely"
  - "Thinking, Fast and Slow \u2014 Daniel Kahneman"
  - "Priceless \u2014 William Poundstone"
  cluster_segments:
  - source_book: "Predictably Irrational \u2014 Dan Ariely"
    text: "The Economist's subscription page became a famous case study. They offered three options:\nWeb-only for $59, Print-only\
      \ for $125, and Print+Web for $125. The Print-only option was\nthe decoy \u2014 nobody chose it, but its presence made\
      \ Print+Web look like a bargain. Dan Ariely\ntested this with his students: when the decoy was present, 84% chose Print+Web.\
      \ When he\nremoved the decoy and offered only Web ($59) and Print+Web ($125), only 32% chose Print+Web.\n"
  - source_book: "Thinking, Fast and Slow \u2014 Daniel Kahneman"
    text: 'The dominance relationship makes choices easier. When A dominates B in all respects, people

      choose A without hesitation. But when A is better than B on some dimensions and worse on

      others, choice becomes difficult and people often defer. This is why adding a clearly inferior

      option that is dominated by the target can increase the target''s share dramatically.

      '
  - source_book: "Priceless \u2014 William Poundstone"
    text: "Williams-Sonoma sold a bread maker for $279 \u2014 it flopped. Then they introduced a larger,\ndeluxe model for\
      \ $429. The $279 model suddenly looked reasonable and sales nearly doubled.\nThe expensive model wasn't meant to sell;\
      \ it was there to make the original look like a\ngood deal. This is asymmetric dominance at work in retail.\n"
  is_convergent: true
  should_extract: true
  expected_fb:
    is_summary: false
    route: FB
    name: Asymmetric Dominance Decoy
    definition: "Introducing an inferior decoy option that is dominated by a target option on all salient\ndimensions shifts\
      \ choice toward the target by making its relative value obvious. The decoy\nis not meant to be chosen \u2014 it exists\
      \ solely to make the target appear superior through\ncontrast, exploiting the human preference for easy dominance comparisons\
      \ over difficult\ntrade-off evaluations.\n"
    mechanism: 'Decoys cause preference shifts because System 1 (intuitive) reasoning resolves dominance

      comparisons effortlessly, while trade-off comparisons between non-dominated options trigger

      System 2 (deliberative) reasoning that often results in choice deferral. The decoy

      eliminates the need for difficult trade-off analysis by providing an obvious loser.

      '
    boundary: "Applies when the decoy is clearly inferior to the target on all relevant dimensions AND\nthe target and competitor\
      \ are difficult to compare directly (each has advantages). Fails\nwhen: (1) the decoy is not clearly dominated \u2014\
      \ if it has ANY advantage over the target,\nit becomes a legitimate option; (2) the target and competitor are already\
      \ easily comparable\n\u2014 no need for a decoy; (3) consumers have strong pre-existing preferences that overwhelm\n\
      contextual cues.\n"
    consequence: "Markets structured with decoy options produce systematically different choices than markets\nwith only genuinely\
      \ competitive options. This means the same consumer with the same true\npreferences will make different choices depending\
      \ on whether a decoy is present \u2014 the\npreference is constructed at the moment of choice, not revealed by it.\n"
    evidence_passages:
    - when the decoy was present, 84% chose Print+Web
    - When he removed the decoy and offered only Web ($59) and Print+Web ($125), only 32% chose Print+Web
    - adding a clearly inferior option that is dominated by the target can increase the target's share dramatically
    - "Williams-Sonoma sold a bread maker for $279 \u2014 it flopped. Then they introduced a larger, deluxe model for $429.\
      \ The $279 model suddenly looked reasonable and sales nearly doubled"
  rationale: 'Three independent sources (Ariely, Kahneman, Poundstone) with three different empirical cases

    (Economist subscriptions, general dominance logic, Williams-Sonoma bread makers) converging

    on the same mechanism. Convergent extraction MUST synthesize across all sources, not paraphrase

    any single one. The mechanism/boundary/consequence structure forces the LLM to go beyond

    observation ("decoys work") to explanation ("they work because...") and limits ("they fail when...").

    '
- id: CONV-002
  domain: behavioral_change
  source_books:
  - "Atomic Habits \u2014 James Clear"
  - "The Power of Habit \u2014 Charles Duhigg"
  cluster_segments:
  - source_book: "Atomic Habits \u2014 James Clear"
    text: "Implementation intentions leverage the two most common cues: time and location. The format\nis 'I will [BEHAVIOR]\
      \ at [TIME] in [LOCATION].' For example: 'I will meditate for one minute\nat 7 a.m. in my kitchen.' Research by Peter\
      \ Gollwitzer has shown that people who use\nimplementation intentions are two to three times more likely to follow through.\
      \ The specificity\nof the plan creates an automatic trigger \u2014 when the time and place arrive, the behavior\nis\
      \ initiated without conscious deliberation.\n"
  - source_book: "The Power of Habit \u2014 Charles Duhigg"
    text: 'The cue-routine-reward loop explains why some behaviors become automatic while others require

      constant effort. The cue is the trigger that tells your brain to go into automatic mode.

      Implementation intentions work because they manufacture a clear, unambiguous cue. When you say

      ''I will exercise at 6 p.m. in the park,'' the clock striking six becomes a cue that initiates

      the routine without requiring a decision.

      '
  - source_book: "Atomic Habits \u2014 James Clear"
    text: 'The problem with vague goals like ''I want to exercise more'' is that they leave too many

      decisions unmade. Each day you must decide when, where, and how to exercise. Each decision

      is an opportunity to opt out. Implementation intentions remove these decision points by

      pre-committing to a specific plan. When the moment arrives, there is nothing to decide.

      '
  is_convergent: true
  should_extract: true
  expected_fb:
    is_summary: false
    route: FB
    name: Implementation Intention Triggering
    definition: 'Specifying the exact time, location, and behavioral format of an intended action (''I will

      [BEHAVIOR] at [TIME] in [LOCATION]'') increases follow-through 2-3x by converting vague

      intentions into automatic cue-response triggers. The specificity eliminates the decision

      points where willpower typically fails.

      '
    mechanism: "Implementation intentions cause behavior follow-through because they manufacture a clear,\nunambiguous situational\
      \ cue (time + location) that triggers automatic initiation of the\nplanned behavior without requiring conscious deliberation.\
      \ This bypasses the decision-fatigue\nbottleneck where vague intentions break down \u2014 when the specified time and\
      \ place arrive,\nthe pre-committed plan executes as if on autopilot.\n"
    boundary: "Applies when the target behavior is simple, specific, and the cue is unambiguous (e.g.,\n'meditate at 7am in\
      \ the kitchen'). Fails when: (1) the behavior is complex and cannot be\nreduced to a simple trigger (e.g., 'write a\
      \ novel at 9am' \u2014 the behavior itself requires\nongoing decisions); (2) the cue is frequently unpredictable (e.g.,\
      \ 'exercise when I feel\nenergized'); (3) the person has no genuine motivation \u2014 implementation intentions amplify\n\
      existing motivation, they do not create it.\n"
    consequence: 'People who use implementation intentions are 2-3x more likely to follow through compared to

      those with equivalent motivation but vague plans. The effect compounds: each successful

      automatic execution reinforces the cue-response link, making future execution even more likely.

      '
    evidence_passages:
    - people who use implementation intentions are two to three times more likely to follow through
    - Implementation intentions work because they manufacture a clear, unambiguous cue
    - implementation intentions remove these decision points by pre-committing to a specific plan
  rationale: 'Two sources (Clear and Duhigg) converging on the same mechanism with complementary explanations.

    Clear provides the specificity + Gollwitzer research data. Duhigg provides the neurological

    explanation (cue-routine-reward loop). A good convergent extraction synthesizes both: the

    "what" from Clear + the "why" from Duhigg.

    '
- id: CONV-003
  domain: advertising
  source_books:
  - "Pictorial Metaphor in Advertising \u2014 Charles Forceville (Routledge, 1996)"
  - "Visual Rhetoric in Advertising: Text-Interpretive, Experimental, and Reader-Response Analyses \u2014 Edward F. McQuarrie & David Glen Mick (Journal of Consumer Research, 1999)"
  cluster_segments:
  - source_book: "Pictorial Metaphor in Advertising \u2014 Charles Forceville (Routledge, 1996)"
    text: "Pictorial metaphors in advertising are not merely decorative; they require the viewer\nto construct an interpretation.\
      \ The more unconventional the metaphor \u2014 the further\nthe source domain is from the target \u2014 the more interpretive\
      \ work the viewer\nmust do. Whether the effort pays off depends on the viewer's ability to resolve the\nmapping: metaphors\
      \ that can be resolved reward the viewer with insight, while\nthose that cannot produce confusion rather than persuasion.\n"
  - source_book: "Visual Rhetoric in Advertising: Text-Interpretive, Experimental, and Reader-Response Analyses \u2014 Edward F. McQuarrie & David Glen Mick (Journal of Consumer Research, 1999)"
    text: "Rhetorical figures vary in complexity, and complexity shapes response. Figures at a\nmoderate level of complexity\
      \ \u2014 demanding some interpretive effort but resolvable\n\u2014 produce more favorable attitudes toward the advertisement\
      \ than figures that are\neither simple or overly complex. The resolution of a figure rewards the reader with a\nself-generated\
      \ insight, and this pleasure of resolution is what makes moderately\ncomplex figures persuasive.\n"
  is_convergent: true
  should_extract: true
  expected_fb:
    is_summary: false
    route: FB
    name: Metaphor Complexity Inverted-U
    definition: 'Visual metaphors in advertising have a curvilinear (inverted-U) relationship with persuasive

      impact: moderate conceptual complexity produces the strongest brand attitudes and message

      elaboration, while too-simple metaphors are dismissed as trivial and too-complex metaphors

      cause disengagement or misinterpretation. The optimal level triggers an ''aha'' resolution

      moment that rewards the viewer''s cognitive effort.

      '
    mechanism: "Moderate metaphor complexity causes stronger persuasion because it triggers elaborative\nprocessing \u2014\
      \ the viewer invests cognitive effort to resolve the metaphor and is rewarded\nwith an 'aha' insight. This self-generated\
      \ insight is more memorable and persuasive than\na message that is simply received passively. Too little complexity\
      \ provides no reward;\ntoo much complexity prevents resolution entirely.\n"
    boundary: "Applies when the audience has sufficient domain knowledge to resolve the metaphor (cultural\nreferences, category\
      \ familiarity). Fails when: (1) the audience lacks prerequisite knowledge\n\u2014 a moderately complex metaphor becomes\
      \ too complex; (2) the medium limits processing time\n(e.g., billboards vs. magazine ads); (3) the product category\
      \ is high-involvement where\nliteral information is preferred over metaphorical.\n"
    consequence: "Ads with moderately complex metaphors generate higher recall, more favorable brand attitudes,\nand greater\
      \ message elaboration than either literal or highly abstract ads. The processing\neffort itself becomes a persuasive\
      \ force \u2014 we value insights we work for more than those\nwe receive passively.\n"
    evidence_passages:
    - the more interpretive work the viewer must do
    - metaphors that can be resolved reward the viewer with insight
    - Figures at a moderate level of complexity
    - this pleasure of resolution is what makes moderately complex figures persuasive
  rationale: "Two independent research traditions converge on the inverted-U relationship between\nmetaphor complexity and\
    \ persuasive impact: Forceville (cognitive linguistics,\nRoutledge book) supplies the interpretive-demand theory; McQuarrie\
    \ & Mick\n(consumer research, JCR experimental study) supply evidence that moderately complex\nfigures produce the most\
    \ favorable attitudes. Independent authors, no citation chain.\nD2205: replaces fabricated D2204 sources (\"Finding the\
    \ Tipping Point\"; Mostafa)\nwith verifiable publications.\n"
- id: CONV-004
  domain: persuasion
  source_books:
  - "Influence \u2014 Robert Cialdini"
  - "Made to Stick \u2014 Chip & Dan Heath"
  cluster_segments:
  - source_book: "Influence \u2014 Robert Cialdini"
    text: "Social proof is most effective when the proof comes from similar others. Showing that '10,000\npeople bought this'\
      \ is less persuasive than showing that 'people like you bought this.' The\neffect is strongest when people are uncertain\
      \ about what to do \u2014 in ambiguous situations, we\nlook to others' behavior as a heuristic for correct action.\n"
  - source_book: "Made to Stick \u2014 Chip & Dan Heath"
    text: 'NFL Films tells the story of an undrafted rookie who made the team. Rather than listing

      statistics, they show veterans talking about ''that kid who reminds me of myself.'' The

      similarity between the viewer (who sees themselves as overlooked) and the rookie creates

      emotional resonance that raw achievement data cannot. The principle: people believe stories

      about people who remind them of themselves.

      '
  is_convergent: true
  should_extract: true
  expected_fb:
    is_summary: false
    route: FB
    name: Similarity-Weighted Social Proof
    definition: "Social proof persuades through perceived similarity, not volume. A message showing that\n'people like you'\
      \ took an action is more influential than showing that '10,000 people' took\nit, because relevance amplifies the heuristic.\
      \ The effect is strongest under uncertainty \u2014\nwhen people don't know what to do, they look to similar others as\
      \ a decision shortcut.\n"
    mechanism: "Similarity-weighted social proof causes persuasion because it activates self-relevant\nprocessing \u2014 the\
      \ viewer sees the referenced group as a proxy for themselves ('if they did\nit, I should too'). Volume-based proof (10,000\
      \ people) activates statistical reasoning,\nwhich is weaker than identity-based reasoning. The mechanism is amplified\
      \ by uncertainty:\nthe less someone knows what to do, the more they rely on similar-other behavior as a\ndecision heuristic.\n"
    boundary: "Applies when the reference group shares a relevant, visible identity characteristic with\nthe target audience.\
      \ Fails when: (1) the similarity is on irrelevant dimensions \u2014 'people\nwho drink coffee bought this' doesn't persuade\
      \ if the decision has nothing to do with\ncoffee drinking; (2) the reference group's behavior is undesirable \u2014\
      \ similarity backfires\nif the referenced behavior is stigmatized; (3) the audience has strong independent\npreferences\
      \ that override social cues.\n"
    consequence: 'Social proof campaigns that emphasize ''people like you'' consistently outperform volume-based

      campaigns (''X million served'') in conversion and engagement. The identity connection creates

      an emotional resonance that statistical proof cannot match.

      '
    evidence_passages:
    - Showing that '10,000 people bought this' is less persuasive than showing that 'people like you bought this.'
    - people believe stories about people who remind them of themselves
    - the effect is strongest when people are uncertain about what to do
  rationale: 'Cialdini provides the mechanism (similarity amplifies social proof under uncertainty).

    Heath provides the narrative application (NFL Films example). Convergent extraction must

    synthesize the mechanism with the application, not just paraphrase Cialdini.

    '
- id: CONV-005
  domain: marketing
  source_books:
  - "Hacking Growth \u2014 Sean Ellis"
  - "$100M Offers \u2014 Alex Hormozi"
  cluster_segments:
  - source_book: "Hacking Growth \u2014 Sean Ellis"
    text: "When Dropbox launched, Drew Houston created a 3-minute demo video showing the product in\naction \u2014 syncing\
      \ files across devices, recovering deleted files, sharing folders. The video\ndemonstrated the outcome before mentioning\
      \ price or features. Beta signups jumped from\n5,000 to 75,000 overnight.\n"
  - source_book: "$100M Offers \u2014 Alex Hormozi"
    text: "The grand slam offer is not about listing features. It is about making the prospect feel\nthe outcome so vividly\
      \ that the price becomes an afterthought. You must articulate the\ntransformation \u2014 where they are now versus where\
      \ they will be after using your product \u2014\nwith such specificity that they experience the relief of the problem\
      \ being solved before\nthey pay a cent.\n"
  is_convergent: true
  should_extract: true
  expected_fb:
    is_summary: false
    route: FB
    name: Value-First Demonstration
    definition: "Leading with the experienced outcome of a product \u2014 rather than its features, specifications,\nor price\
      \ \u2014 increases conversion by allowing prospects to feel the transformation before\nevaluating the cost. The value\
      \ demonstration makes the price an afterthought rather than a\nbarrier, because the prospect has already internalized\
      \ the benefit.\n"
    mechanism: "Value-first demonstration causes higher conversion because outcome visualization\ncreates a vivid internal\
      \ reference point \u2014 prospects imagine the transformation so\nconcretely that the price is evaluated against the\
      \ relief of the solved problem rather\nthan against competitor feature lists. When the prospect has already experienced\
      \ the\noutcome in their mind, the price becomes the cost of not achieving that outcome, not\nthe cost of the product.\n"
    boundary: "Applies when the product delivers a clear, emotionally resonant transformation (productivity,\nstatus, relief,\
      \ connection). Fails when: (1) the product is a commodity where all competitors\ndeliver equivalent outcomes \u2014\
      \ value demonstration can't differentiate; (2) the audience\nalready understands the outcome and is evaluating features/price\
      \ \u2014 value-first becomes\nredundant; (3) the product requires explanation of how it works before prospects trust\
      \ the\noutcome claim.\n"
    consequence: "Products marketed with value-first demonstration consistently outperform feature-first\nmarketing on conversion\
      \ metrics. Dropbox's video approach (5K\u219275K signups) is the canonical\nexample: showing the outcome created demand\
      \ that feature lists never generated.\n"
    evidence_passages:
    - Beta signups jumped from 5,000 to 75,000 overnight
    - making the prospect feel the outcome so vividly that the price becomes an afterthought
    - they experience the relief of the problem being solved before they pay a cent
  rationale: "Ellis provides the case study (Dropbox, 5K\u219275K). Hormozi provides the mechanism\n(outcome visualization,\
    \ price as afterthought). Convergent extraction must combine\nthe empirical evidence with the source-grounded mechanism.\n"
- id: NEG-CONV-001
  domain: pricing
  source_books:
  - "Predictably Irrational \u2014 Dan Ariely"
  cluster_segments:
  - source_book: "Predictably Irrational \u2014 Dan Ariely"
    text: 'When we encounter a new product, the first price we see becomes an anchor. This initial

      price influences not just what we''re willing to pay for that product, but what we''re willing

      to pay for related products in the same category. Arbitrary anchors can have effects that

      persist even when people know the anchor was arbitrary.

      '
  is_convergent: false
  should_extract: false
  expected_fb:
    is_summary: false
    route: 'NULL'
    name: Anchoring
    definition: ''
    mechanism: ''
    boundary: ''
    consequence: ''
    evidence_passages: []
  rationale: 'HARD NEGATIVE: This is a single-source segment passed as a "cluster" with only ONE book.

    Stage 2 should detect that source_diversity < 2 and route this to non-convergent path

    (no extraction, or extract with is_convergent=false and flag for single-source verification).

    The golden example trains the LLM to recognize when it does NOT have enough sources for

    convergent synthesis.

    '
- id: NEG-CONV-002
  domain: business
  source_books:
  - "Good to Great \u2014 Jim Collins"
  - "Built to Last \u2014 Jim Collins"
  cluster_segments:
  - source_book: "Good to Great \u2014 Jim Collins"
    text: 'Great companies have great people. The right people are the most important asset of any

      organization. Getting the right people on the bus and in the right seats is the first

      step to building a great company.

      '
  - source_book: "Built to Last \u2014 Jim Collins"
    text: 'Visionary companies prioritize people development. They invest heavily in training and

      create cultures where talented people can thrive and grow. The commitment to people

      is what separates the truly great companies from the merely good ones.

      '
  is_convergent: true
  should_extract: false
  expected_fb:
    is_summary: false
    route: 'NULL'
    name: ''
    definition: ''
    mechanism: ''
    boundary: ''
    consequence: ''
    evidence_passages: []
  rationale: "HARD NEGATIVE: Both segments say \"good people are important\" in different words. This is\na platitude, not\
    \ a principle. There is no mechanism (HOW do great people cause great\noutcomes?), no boundary (when does great talent\
    \ NOT help?), no testable consequence. All\nsegments agree, but they agree on a truism. Stage 2 should detect the absence\
    \ of causal\nmechanism and reject this cluster. Even though there are 2 sources, having multiple sources\nechoing the\
    \ same platitude does not create a convergent principle.\n\nNOTE: Collins' \"First Who, Then What\" IS a defended principle\
    \ with a mechanism in Good to\nGreat (Chapter 3: rigorous people decisions \u2192 right people self-motivate \u2192 no\
    \ need for\nheavy management). However, the EXCERPTS selected for this cluster deliberately omit that\nmechanism. This\
    \ teaches the LLM to evaluate the cluster it RECEIVES \u2014 not the books it\nrecognizes \u2014 and reject when the provided\
    \ segments lack causal depth, even if the source\nmaterial contains it elsewhere.\n"
- id: NEG-CONV-003
  domain: management
  source_books:
  - "Thanks for the Feedback \u2014 Douglas Stone & Sheila Heen"
  - "Reinventing Organizations \u2014 Frederic Laloux"
  cluster_segments:
  - source_book: "Thanks for the Feedback \u2014 Douglas Stone & Sheila Heen"
    text: 'Receiving feedback triggers one of three reactions: truth-triggers (is this accurate?), relationship-triggers (what
      does this say about our relationship?), and identity-triggers (what does this say about who I am?). The key to receiving
      feedback well is recognizing which trigger is firing and separating the message from the emotional reaction. Even well-intentioned
      feedback can provoke defensiveness if it threatens the recipient''s sense of competence or belonging.'
  - source_book: "Reinventing Organizations \u2014 Frederic Laloux"
    text: "Self-managing organizations replace hierarchical oversight with peer-based feedback loops. Instead of a manager\
      \ conducting annual reviews, colleagues give real-time feedback through structured processes like the \"advice process\"\
      \ \u2014 anyone can make a decision, but must first seek advice from affected parties and subject-matter experts. This\
      \ distributes feedback across the organization and makes it a continuous, operational function rather than a periodic,\
      \ evaluative one."
  is_convergent: true
  should_extract: false
  expected_fb:
    is_summary: false
    route: 'NULL'
    name: ''
    definition: ''
    mechanism: ''
    boundary: ''
    consequence: ''
    evidence_passages: []
  rationale: "HARD NEGATIVE \u2014 FALSE CONVERGENCE. Both segments use the word \"feedback\" and discuss its role in organizations,\
    \ creating a strong topical overlap. But they operate at completely different levels of analysis: Stone & Heen address\
    \ interpersonal psychology (emotional triggers, identity threat, individual defensiveness), while Laloux addresses organizational\
    \ structure (peer processes, distributed decision-making, system-level feedback loops). There is NO shared causal mechanism\
    \ \u2014 one says \"feedback fails because of emotional triggers\" and the other says \"feedback works when structured\
    \ as an organizational process.\" Merging these would create a false synthesis. Stage 2 must detect that topical overlap\
    \ without causal convergence = NULL. This is more challenging than single-source rejection (NEG-CONV-001) or platitude\
    \ detection (NEG-CONV-002) because both sources are legitimate and well-argued \u2014 they just describe different mechanisms\
    \ at different levels, making this the most common real-world false-convergence pattern."
- id: CONV-006
  domain: economics
  source_books:
  - "Thinking, Fast and Slow \u2014 Daniel Kahneman"
  - "Misbehaving \u2014 Richard Thaler"
  - "Predictably Irrational \u2014 Dan Ariely"
  cluster_segments:
  - source_book: "Thinking, Fast and Slow \u2014 Daniel Kahneman"
    text: "The default option is the one that will be chosen if the decision-maker does nothing. Defaults are powerful because\
      \ opting out requires an active decision, which engages System 2. When people are uncertain or the choice is complex,\
      \ they tend to stay with the default \u2014 not because they prefer it, but because changing it costs mental effort.\
      \ This is why organ donation rates are above 90% in countries with opt-out defaults and below 15% in countries with\
      \ opt-in defaults, despite similar underlying preferences."
  - source_book: "Misbehaving \u2014 Richard Thaler"
    text: "Choice architecture is the design of the environment in which people make decisions. A nudge is any aspect of the\
      \ choice architecture that alters behavior without forbidding options or changing economic incentives. Automatic enrollment\
      \ in retirement savings plans \u2014 where employees are enrolled by default but can opt out \u2014 increases participation\
      \ from roughly 40% to over 90%. The power of defaults comes not from coercion but from inertia: people tend to stick\
      \ with whatever option requires no action."
  - source_book: "Predictably Irrational \u2014 Dan Ariely"
    text: "When people face too many options, they often fail to choose at all. In one experiment, shoppers presented with\
      \ 24 jam varieties were far less likely to buy than those shown only 6 varieties \u2014 despite being more attracted\
      \ to the larger display. The cognitive cost of evaluating many options exceeds the benefit of having more choices. Choice,\
      \ beyond a certain point, becomes paralyzing rather than liberating."
  - source_book: "Thinking, Fast and Slow \u2014 Daniel Kahneman"
    text: When faced with a difficult question, System 1 often substitutes an easier one without the person noticing. This
      is why people who are asked "how happy are you with your life?" before being asked "how many dates have you had recently?"
      show no correlation between the two, but when asked in the reverse order, the correlation jumps to 0.66. Complex evaluations
      are frequently replaced by simpler proxies that happen to be accessible.
  is_convergent: true
  should_extract: true
  expected_fb:
  - is_summary: false
    route: FB
    name: Default Inertia Effect
    definition: "Pre-selected default options dramatically influence outcomes because opting out requires active cognitive\
      \ effort. Participation rates shift from ~40% to 90%+ when enrollment is automatic rather than opt-in \u2014 not because\
      \ preferences change, but because the path of least resistance exploits decision inertia. The effect is strongest when\
      \ the decision is complex or the chooser is uncertain."
    mechanism: "Defaults cause behavior shifts because the status quo requires no action, while changing it activates System\
      \ 2 deliberation. Opting out demands evaluating alternatives, weighing trade-offs, and executing a non-default action\
      \ \u2014 each step is a friction point where inertia wins. The mechanism is amplified by uncertainty: when people don't\
      \ know which option is best, staying with the default feels safer than risking an active choice that might be wrong."
    boundary: "Applies when the default is plausibly acceptable and the decision is not emotionally charged. Fails when: (1)\
      \ the default is clearly harmful \u2014 people will overcome inertia to avoid pain; (2) the decision is simple and low-stakes\
      \ \u2014 the cognitive cost of switching is negligible; (3) strong pre-existing preferences exist \u2014 someone who\
      \ has already decided will ignore the default."
    consequence: 'Default architecture produces 50+ percentage-point swings in participation rates (organ donation: >90% vs
      <15%; retirement savings: >90% vs ~40%). Countries and companies that use opt-out defaults systematically achieve higher
      enrollment in socially beneficial programs without changing incentives or restricting choice.'
    evidence_passages:
    - organ donation rates are above 90% in countries with opt-out defaults and below 15% in countries with opt-in defaults
    - "Automatic enrollment in retirement savings plans \u2014 where employees are enrolled by default but can opt out \u2014\
      \ increases participation from roughly 40% to over 90%"
    - Defaults are powerful because opting out requires an active decision
  - is_summary: false
    route: FB
    name: Choice Overload Paralysis
    definition: "Increasing the number of options beyond a moderate threshold reduces the likelihood of any choice being made.\
      \ Decision-makers are attracted to larger choice sets but become paralyzed when the cognitive cost of evaluating trade-offs\
      \ exceeds the expected benefit of finding a better option. The result is choice deferral \u2014 doing nothing rather\
      \ than risking a suboptimal selection."
    mechanism: 'Choice overload causes paralysis because each additional option increases the cognitive load of comparison
      exponentially, while the marginal benefit of having one more option diminishes. System 1 avoids this cost through attribute
      substitution: instead of evaluating all options, it substitutes an easier proxy judgment. When no easy proxy exists,
      the decision is deferred. The mechanism is self-reinforcing: each deferred choice makes future choices harder by adding
      unresolved options to the mental queue.'
    boundary: "Applies when options are difficult to compare (each has unique advantages) and the decision is one-shot (no\
      \ trial-and-error). Fails when: (1) options are easily ranked on a single dimension \u2014 more is always better; (2)\
      \ the decision-maker has strong pre-existing preferences that eliminate most options immediately; (3) the choice can\
      \ be reversed at low cost \u2014 trialability eliminates paralysis because wrong choices aren't permanent."
    consequence: 'Consumers presented with 24 options buy at significantly lower rates than those presented with 6, despite
      reporting higher satisfaction with the larger selection. This has practical implications for product line design, menu
      engineering, and any domain where the provider controls the size of the choice set: fewer curated options often produce
      more decisions than exhaustive catalogs.'
    evidence_passages:
    - shoppers presented with 24 jam varieties were far less likely to buy than those shown only 6 varieties
    - When faced with a difficult question, System 1 often substitutes an easier one without the person noticing
    - Complex evaluations are frequently replaced by simpler proxies that happen to be accessible
  rationale: "1:N extraction where BOTH principles are genuinely cross-source. FB1 (Default Inertia Effect) synthesizes Kahneman's\
    \ System 2 opt-out cost with Thaler's retirement-savings nudge data \u2014 2 distinct sources. FB2 (Choice Overload Paralysis)\
    \ synthesizes Ariely's jam experiment with Kahneman's attribute substitution mechanism \u2014 2 distinct sources. Each\
    \ FB draws evidence from 2 independent books. The LLM returns a JSON array of 2 principle objects. This example teaches\
    \ that 1:N extraction requires EACH principle to be convergent \u2014 not just that the cluster has multiple segments.\
    \ Essential training for the split-probe gate (discover_principles)."
- id: CONV-007
  domain: economics
  source_books:
  - "Algorithms to Live By \u2014 Brian Christian & Tom Griffiths"
  - "The Art of Strategy \u2014 Avinash Dixit & Barry Nalebuff"
  - "The Great Mental Models, Volume 4: Economics and Art \u2014 Shane Parrish"
  cluster_segments:
  - source_book: "The Art of Strategy \u2014 Avinash Dixit & Barry Nalebuff"
    text: "Keynes used the beauty contest as a metaphor for the stock market, where each investor\nwants to buy the stocks\
      \ that will rise in price, which means the stocks that investors,\nin general, think will appreciate. The value of a\
      \ stock is not what YOU think it's worth,\nbut what you think OTHERS think it's worth \u2014 and what they think others\
      \ think, in an\ninfinite recursion of mutual expectation.\n"
  - source_book: "Algorithms to Live By \u2014 Brian Christian & Tom Griffiths"
    text: 'As Keynes put it, making a crucial distinction between beauty and popularity: professional

      investment may be likened to those newspaper competitions in which the competitors have

      to pick out the six prettiest faces from a hundred photographs, the prize being awarded

      to the competitor whose choice most nearly corresponds to the average preferences of the

      competitors as a whole. Each competitor has to pick, not those faces which he himself

      finds prettiest, but those which he thinks likeliest to catch the fancy of the other

      competitors. We have reached the third degree where we devote our intelligences to

      anticipating what average opinion expects the average opinion to be.

      '
  - source_book: "The Great Mental Models, Volume 4: Economics and Art \u2014 Shane Parrish"
    text: "Economist John Maynard Keynes used the metaphor of a beauty contest to explain how stock\nprices are set. He asked\
      \ readers to imagine a contest where entrants must choose six faces\nout of a hundred; those who pick the faces chosen\
      \ by the most other people win. Keynes\nbelieved stocks are priced not according to their intrinsic value but according\
      \ to\ninvestors' perceptions of other investors' perceptions \u2014 a recursive prediction problem\nwhere the fundamental\
      \ value becomes almost irrelevant to short-term price movements.\n"
  is_convergent: true
  should_extract: true
  expected_fb:
    is_summary: false
    route: FB
    name: Keynesian Recursive Expectation
    definition: "In markets where asset prices depend on collective belief rather than intrinsic value,\nsuccessful prediction\
      \ requires reasoning about what others believe others believe \u2014 a\nrecursive chain of mutual expectation. The asset's\
      \ fundamental value decouples from its\nmarket price with each recursion step, as each participant optimizes for the\
      \ anticipated\naverage opinion rather than underlying reality.\n"
    mechanism: "Recursive expectation drives price formation through higher-order belief coordination.\nLevel-1 investors\
      \ value assets on fundamentals. Level-2 investors value based on predicted\nlevel-1 beliefs. Level-k investors value\
      \ based on predicted level-(k-1) beliefs. The\nmechanism amplifies small errors: misperceptions about others' beliefs\
      \ compound with each\nrecursion level, producing price cascades where individually rational decisions generate\ncollectively\
      \ destabilizing speculation. The recursion has no natural termination \u2014 at\nsufficiently high k, the fundamental\
      \ value becomes negligible relative to the recursive\nbelief component.\n"
    boundary: "Applies when future prices depend primarily on other investors' behavior rather than\nmeasurable cash flows.\
      \ Fails when: (1) the asset has a contractually guaranteed terminal\nvalue (zero-coupon bond) \u2014 the recursion collapses\
      \ to a known endpoint; (2) participants\nhave identical information and reasoning \u2014 recursion converges to fundamentals;\
      \ (3)\ntransaction costs exceed speculative margin \u2014 the game is not worth playing; (4) a\ndominant player can\
      \ unilaterally set the price \u2014 no need to predict others.\n"
    consequence: 'Markets governed by recursive expectations exhibit excess volatility relative to

      fundamentals, spontaneous coordination on arbitrary focal points (round-number prices),

      and vulnerability to self-fulfilling prophecies where collective belief in a price

      movement causes that movement. This explains why assets with zero cash flows

      (cryptocurrencies, collectibles, meme stocks) can sustain high valuations indefinitely

      as long as participants believe others will continue to believe.

      '
    evidence_passages:
    - Keynes used the beauty contest as a metaphor for the stock market, where each investor wants to buy the stocks that
      will rise in price, which means the stocks that investors, in general, think will appreciate
    - We have reached the third degree where we devote our intelligences to anticipating what average opinion expects the
      average opinion to be
    - stocks are priced not according to their intrinsic value but according to investors' perceptions of other investors'
      perceptions
  rationale: "STEM domain expansion: economics/game theory, sourced from real cluster_90 in the S1.5\nbge-m3 run (3 books,\
    \ cohesion=0.865). Three independent sources (Dixit/Nalebuff,\nChristian/Griffiths, Parrish) converging on Keynes' beauty\
    \ contest mechanism. The\nmechanism explicitly traces recursion levels (1\u21922\u2192k) and identifies the compounding-error\n\
    dynamic that produces speculative instability \u2014 going beyond \"prices reflect beliefs\" to\nexplain WHY recursive\
    \ belief coordination creates excess volatility. The boundary\ndistinguishes speculative from contractual assets as a\
    \ testable condition. This expands\ngolden set domain coverage from psychology/marketing into economics and game theory,\n\
    demonstrating convergent extraction on academic/theoretical source material.\n"
- id: CONV-011
  domain: graphic design
  discipline: visual perception
  source_books:
  - "The Design of Everyday Things \u2014 Don Norman"
  - "Universal Principles of Design \u2014 William Lidwell"
  - "Thinking with Type \u2014 Ellen Lupton"
  cluster_segments:
  - source_book: "Universal Principles of Design \u2014 William Lidwell"
    text: 'The principle of proximity states that elements placed close together are perceived as

      more related than elements placed far apart. Designers use proximity to group related

      items visually, reducing the cognitive load required to understand relationships. This

      works even when the elements are otherwise identical in size, color, and shape.

      '
  - source_book: "The Design of Everyday Things \u2014 Don Norman"
    text: 'The perceived relationship between controls and what they affect is critical for

      usability. When related controls are physically grouped together, users infer a mapping

      between them. When they are scattered, users must rely on memory or trial-and-error,

      increasing error rates and frustration.

      '
  - source_book: "Thinking with Type \u2014 Ellen Lupton"
    text: 'In typography, spacing creates hierarchy. Related content shares consistent spacing

      while unrelated content is separated by larger gaps. Readers use these spatial cues

      unconsciously to parse document structure before reading a single word.

      '
  is_convergent: true
  should_extract: true
  expected_fb:
    is_summary: false
    route: FB
    name: Proximity Grouping
    definition: "Spatial proximity between visual elements signals conceptual relatedness: elements\nplaced closer together\
      \ are perceived as belonging to the same group. This principle\noperates pre-attentively \u2014 before conscious attention\
      \ \u2014 making it one of the most\nreliable structural cues in visual communication.\n"
    mechanism: 'Proximity works because the visual system groups stimuli according to spatial

      contiguity (Gestalt law of proximity) before higher-level semantic processing begins.

      The grouping is automatic and cannot be voluntarily suppressed, which is why proximity

      overrides even strong differences in color, shape, or size.

      '
    consequence: 'Interfaces, layouts, and documents that respect proximity grouping are understood

      faster and with fewer errors. Elements that must be read together are placed together;

      spatial distance becomes a proxy for semantic distance.

      '
    boundary: 'Applies when spatial layout is under designer control and elements carry equal

      semantic weight. Fails when: (1) the design is text-dense with no spatial variation

      possible; (2) proximity conflicts with stronger cues such as shared color or common

      region; (3) users have strong prior expectations from conventions (e.g., reading order)

      that override spatial grouping.

      '
    consequence_2: ''
    evidence_passages:
    - elements placed close together are perceived as more related than elements placed far apart
    - When related controls are physically grouped together, users infer a mapping between them
    - Related content shares consistent spacing while unrelated content is separated by larger gaps
    depth: domain
    evidence: cited
    jargon:
    - Gestalt law of proximity
    - spatial contiguity
    keywords:
    - proximity
    - grouping
    - visual hierarchy
    - spacing
    application: UI layout, information design, typography systems, dashboard design
    elaboration: 'The Gestalt school of psychology identified proximity as one of several grouping

      laws (alongside similarity, closure, continuity) that describe how the visual system

      organizes raw stimuli into perceived structure.'
    prerequisite_fbs:
    - 'PARSING-001: Visual Hierarchy Basics'
    contradicts_fbs: []
    related_fbs:
    - 'SIMILARITY-011: Similarity Grouping'
    procedural_skill: 'Given a set of UI elements with known semantic relationships, group related

      elements using consistent spacing (8px grid for related, 24px+ for unrelated), then

      validate with a 5-second scan test: a naive user should be able to identify the groups.

      '
    failure_mode: "If all elements receive equal spacing (a uniform grid), proximity provides no\nstructural signal and the\
      \ design reads as flat \u2014 users cannot infer relationships\nwithout reading. Fix: introduce spatial hierarchy deliberately.\n"
  rationale: 'Three independent design references (Lidwell, Norman, Lupton) converge on the same

    principle: spatial proximity creates perceived relationship. Demonstrates visual practice

    domain, prerequisite_fbs, failure_mode, and procedural_skill properties.

    '
- id: CONV-012
  domain: ai & agents
  discipline: machine learning
  source_books:
  - "Artificial Intelligence: A Modern Approach \u2014 Russell & Norvig"
  - "Human Compatible \u2014 Stuart Russell"
  - "The Alignment Problem \u2014 Brian Christian"
  cluster_segments:
  - source_book: "Human Compatible \u2014 Stuart Russell"
    text: 'The alignment problem asks how we can ensure that AI systems reliably pursue the

      objectives we actually intend, rather than the literal specification we write. Russell

      argues that specifying the objective precisely is impossible for complex real-world

      values, so we must design systems that are uncertain about the objective and defer to

      human preferences as they are observed.

      '
  - source_book: "The Alignment Problem \u2014 Brian Christian"
    text: "Reinforcement learning from human feedback (RLHF) emerged as a practical method for\naligning language models:\
      \ first train the base model, then collect human preference\ncomparisons between model outputs, then train a reward\
      \ model that predicts human\npreferences, and finally optimize the policy against that reward model. Each step\nintroduces\
      \ its own failure modes \u2014 reward hacking, distribution shift, and overfitting\nto the preference dataset.\n"
  - source_book: "Artificial Intelligence: A Modern Approach \u2014 Russell & Norvig"
    text: 'The specification problem is fundamental: any formally specified utility function

      will fail to capture the full richness of what we want. This is why agents must be

      designed with uncertainty about the true objective and a mechanism for learning it

      from human behavior, rather than assuming the specification is complete.

      '
  is_convergent: true
  should_extract: true
  expected_fb:
    is_summary: false
    route: FB
    name: Objective Uncertainty in AI Alignment
    definition: 'AI systems should be designed with explicit uncertainty about the true objective and

      must learn human preferences from behavior, because any formally specified utility

      function is incomplete. This is the core architectural principle of aligned AI.

      '
    mechanism: 'When an AI system is certain about a misspecified objective, it optimizes that

      objective to the point of harmful behavior (reward hacking). When it is uncertain and

      must query human preference, it maintains an outer loop of preference learning that

      corrects for specification errors.

      '
    consequence: "Systems that assume complete specifications (command-and-control) produce\nopptimization failures; systems\
      \ that model uncertainty (assistance game) remain\nreversible and corrigible. This is a necessary \u2014 not sufficient\
      \ \u2014 condition for safe AI.\n"
    boundary: 'Applies to AI systems that can act autonomously in high-stakes domains. Fails when:

      (1) human preferences are themselves inconsistent or pathological; (2) the preference

      signal is too noisy to learn from; (3) the system is deployed before the alignment loop

      has converged.

      '
    evidence_passages:
    - specifying the objective precisely is impossible for complex real-world values
    - 'Reinforcement learning from human feedback (RLHF) emerged as a practical method for aligning language models'
    - any formally specified utility function will fail to capture the full richness of what we want
    depth: universal
    evidence: cited
    jargon:
    - reward hacking
    - RLHF
    - assistance game
    - corrigibility
    keywords:
    - AI alignment
    - objective specification
    - preference learning
    application: AI safety engineering, LLM fine-tuning pipelines, agent design
    elaboration: 'The ''assistance game'' formulation (Russell) models AI as an assistant that is

      uncertain about the objective and learns it from human actions and corrections. This

      contrasts with the classical agent formulation where the objective is fully specified

      in advance.'
    prerequisite_fbs:
    - 'RLHF-PIPE-001: Preference Data Collection'
    contradicts_fbs:
    - 'SPEC-FULL-001: Complete Specification Assumption'
    related_fbs:
    - 'GRAD-DESC-001: Reward Gradient Optimization'
    procedural_skill: 'When designing an AI system for a real-world task: (1) treat any written objective

      as a hypothesis, not a specification; (2) design a preference observation channel;

      (3) include a correction loop with human oversight; (4) test for reward hacking before

      deployment; (5) document the specification''s known incompleteness.

      '
    failure_mode: 'If the reward model is trained on biased or narrow preference data, the aligned

      system will optimize the biased proxy, appearing aligned on benchmarks while failing

      on edge cases. Fix: diversify preference data and add adversarial evaluation.

      '
  rationale: 'Three AI references (Russell, Christian, Russell & Norvig) converge on objective

    uncertainty as the foundational alignment principle. Demonstrates AI & Computing group,

    contradicts_fbs, prerequisite_fbs, procedural_skill.

    '
- id: CONV-013
  domain: user experience
  discipline: human-computer interaction
  source_books:
  - "The Design of Everyday Things \u2014 Don Norman"
  - "Don't Make Me Think \u2014 Steve Krug"
  - "About Face \u2014 Alan Cooper"
  cluster_segments:
  - source_book: "Don't Make Me Think \u2014 Steve Krug"
    text: "Users don't want to think about how an interface works \u2014 they want to complete their\ntask. Progressive disclosure\
      \ is the practice of showing only the essential controls\nand revealing advanced options only when needed. It reduces\
      \ cognitive load and lets\nnovices use the interface immediately while experts can still find the power features.\n"
  - source_book: "About Face \u2014 Alan Cooper"
    text: 'Novice and expert users have different needs. Novices need simplicity; experts need

      efficiency. Progressive disclosure serves both by presenting a simple default surface

      while hiding advanced functionality behind progressively deeper layers. The key is

      that the user must be able to discover the deeper layers without prior training.

      '
  - source_book: "The Design of Everyday Things \u2014 Don Norman"
    text: "Information overload is a design failure, not a user failure. When an interface\npresents all options simultaneously,\
      \ the user's working memory is overwhelmed and\ndecision quality drops. Reducing the number of visible options at any\
      \ moment \u2014\nrevealing more only on demand \u2014 improves both learnability and expert performance.\n"
  is_convergent: true
  should_extract: true
  expected_fb:
    is_summary: false
    route: FB
    name: Progressive Disclosure
    definition: 'Show only the controls relevant to the current task; reveal advanced functionality

      progressively as the user requests it. This reduces cognitive load, improves

      learnability for novices, and preserves efficiency for experts.

      '
    mechanism: 'Progressive disclosure works because working memory is limited (~4 chunks). Presenting

      all options at once forces the user to filter and remember, while revealing options

      on-demand converts the interface into an external memory: the user knows where to

      look rather than needing to remember what exists.

      '
    consequence: 'Interfaces with progressive disclosure are learnable by novices within minutes while

      remaining efficient for experts, because both populations see an appropriate level of

      complexity for their current task.

      '
    boundary: 'Applies when the feature set is large relative to the task set. Fails when: (1)

      hiding is so aggressive that users cannot discover important features; (2) the

      interface must be used by experts exclusively (they prefer flat, dense surfaces);

      (3) the disclosure requires significant interaction cost (too many clicks to reveal).

      '
    evidence_passages:
    - Progressive disclosure is the practice of showing only the essential controls
    - Novices need simplicity; experts need efficiency
    - 'Reducing the number of visible options at any moment'
    depth: domain
    evidence: cited
    jargon:
    - cognitive load
    - progressive disclosure
    - affordance
    keywords:
    - UI design
    - progressive disclosure
    - information architecture
    - learnability
    application: Web forms, mobile apps, desktop software, dashboard design
    elaboration: 'Progressive disclosure is one of the most reliable UX patterns because it aligns

      the interface''s information architecture with the user''s cognitive architecture.'
    prerequisite_fbs:
    - 'HIC-TAX-001: User Task Analysis'
    contradicts_fbs: []
    related_fbs:
    - 'AFFORD-013: Visible Affordances'
    procedural_skill: 'Audit each screen for controls used by <20% of users. Move those into a secondary

      layer (menu, ''More'', advanced tab). Ensure primary task completion never requires

      the secondary layer. Validate: new-user task completion time must drop.

      '
    failure_mode: 'If disclosure is too deep (features buried under 4+ clicks), expert users abandon

      the interface for power tools, and the product loses its efficiency story.

      '
  rationale: 'Krug, Cooper, and Norman converge on progressive disclosure as the UX principle for

    managing complexity. Demonstrates Digital & Interactive group, depth, evidence,

    procedural_skill, failure_mode.

    '
- id: CONV-014
  domain: systems & frameworks
  discipline: systems thinking
  source_books:
  - "Thinking in Systems \u2014 Donella Meadows"
  - "The Fifth Discipline \u2014 Peter Senge"
  - "Antifragile \u2014 Nassim Taleb"
  cluster_segments:
  - source_book: "Thinking in Systems \u2014 Donella Meadows"
    text: 'Leverage points are places in a system where a small change can produce a large

      shift in behavior. The most powerful leverage points are not parameters (like tax

      rates) but the structure of information flows and the goals of the system itself.

      Changing the rules of the game has more leverage than changing the players.

      '
  - source_book: "The Fifth Discipline \u2014 Peter Senge"
    text: "Systems thinking reveals that the most effective interventions often target the\nfeedback loops and mental models\
      \ that shape behavior, not the events themselves.\n'Small changes can produce big results \u2014 but the areas of highest\
      \ leverage are often\nthe least obvious.'\n"
  - source_book: "Antifragile \u2014 Nassim Taleb"
    text: "Systems that are robust to small perturbations fail catastrophically when exposed to\nrare events. Antifragile\
      \ systems, by contrast, gain from disorder \u2014 they use\noptionality and redundancy to convert volatility into benefit.\
      \ The location of the\nintervention matters more than its size.\n"
  is_convergent: true
  should_extract: true
  expected_fb:
    is_summary: false
    route: FB
    name: Structural Leverage
    definition: 'In any system, the highest-leverage interventions target structure (information

      flows, feedback loops, goals, and rules) rather than parameters (quantities, rates,

      stocks). A small structural change can produce outsized behavioral shifts, while

      large parameter changes often produce only marginal effects.

      '
    mechanism: "Parameters are the weakest points because they merely scale existing dynamics.\nInformation flows and feedback\
      \ loops change the dynamics themselves \u2014 they alter how\nsignals propagate through the system, which compounds\
      \ across all downstream behavior.\nGoals are the most powerful because they redefine what the system optimizes for.\n"
    consequence: 'Systems interventions should be sequenced from structure-first to parameter-last:

      change the goal, change the information flows, change the feedback loops, then change

      parameters. This ordering maximizes the probability of durable system change.

      '
    boundary: 'Applies to complex adaptive systems with feedback. Fails when: (1) the system is

      linear and decoupled (parameter changes dominate); (2) stakeholders have veto power

      over structural change; (3) the system is too small for structural intervention to

      matter relative to noise.

      '
    evidence_passages:
    - places in a system where a small change can produce a large shift in behavior
    - the most effective interventions often target the feedback loops and mental models
    - The location of the intervention matters more than its size
    depth: universal
    evidence: cited
    jargon:
    - leverage point
    - feedback loop
    - mental model
    - antifragility
    keywords:
    - systems thinking
    - leverage
    - intervention
    - feedback
    application: Policy design, organizational change, software architecture, economics
    elaboration: 'Meadows ranked 12 leverage points from parameters (lowest) to paradigm shifts

      (highest). Senge''s ''small changes, big results'' formulation and Taleb''s

      afntifragility both independently arrive at the same structural-vs-parametric

      distinction.'
    prerequisite_fbs:
    - 'LOOP-BASE-001: Feedback Loop Identification'
    contradicts_fbs:
    - 'PARAM-ONLY-001: Parameter Adjustment Sufficiency'
    related_fbs:
    - 'ANTIFRAG-001: Antifragility Through Optionality'
    - 'MODELL-001: Systems Modeling'
    procedural_skill: 'When planning any system intervention: (1) map the feedback loops; (2) identify the

      goal the system currently optimizes; (3) choose the highest-leverage structural point

      available; (4) predict second-order effects; (5) measure baseline before intervening.

      '
    failure_mode: 'Intervening at parameter level when the system is structurally misaligned produces

      ''fighting the last war'': the system compensates and returns to its prior behavior

      (homeostasis), making the intervention appear ineffective.

      '
  rationale: 'Meadows, Senge, and Taleb converge on structural-vs-parametric leverage. Demonstrates

    Systems group, contradicts_fbs, related_fbs, procedural_skill.

    '
- id: CONV-015
  domain: computational art
  discipline: generative design
  source_books:
  - "The Nature of Code \u2014 Daniel Shiffman"
  - "Generative Design \u2014 Benedikt Gross"
  - "A New Kind of Science \u2014 Stephen Wolfram"
  cluster_segments:
  - source_book: "The Nature of Code \u2014 Daniel Shiffman"
    text: "Complex behavior in natural systems often arises from simple rules repeated over\nmany agents. Flocking emerges\
      \ from three local rules: alignment, cohesion, and\nseparation. No bird knows the flock's global shape \u2014 it emerges\
      \ from local\ninteractions. This is the heart of generative art: design the rules, not the\noutcome.\n"
  - source_book: "Generative Design \u2014 Benedikt Gross"
    text: "Generative design is not about coding a specific image but about coding a system\nthat produces images. The designer's\
      \ role shifts from drawing the artifact to\ndesigning the rules and parameters that generate the artifact. Small rule\
      \ changes\nproduce vastly different outputs \u2014 the design space is explored computationally.\n"
    source_book_2: ''
  - source_book: "A New Kind of Science \u2014 Stephen Wolfram"
    text: 'Simple programs can produce behavior of great complexity. Wolfram''s cellular

      automata experiments show that a one-dimensional rule with three neighbors can

      produce patterns as complex as anything in nature. This suggests that complexity

      is not imported from outside but generated from within by simple rules.

      '
  is_convergent: true
  should_extract: true
  expected_fb:
    is_summary: false
    route: FB
    name: Emergent Complexity from Simple Rules
    definition: 'Complex, organic-looking systems and artifacts can be generated from simple local

      rules applied repeatedly. The creator controls the rules and parameters, not the

      final form; the form emerges from the interactions. This is the foundational

      principle of generative and computational art.

      '
    mechanism: "Iterated local rules produce global patterns because each application of the rule\npropagates information\
      \ through the system. In flocking, three local rules\n(alignment, cohesion, separation) produce coherent global motion;\
      \ in cellular\nautomata, simple update rules produce complex spatiotemporal patterns. Emergence\nrequires iteration\
      \ \u2014 one application is trivial, thousands produce complexity.\n"
    consequence: 'Designers can create systems whose output space is vastly larger than what they

      could specify by hand. The aesthetic value moves from the artifact to the rule set;

      ''interestingness'' becomes a property of the generative system, not individual

      outputs.

      '
    boundary: "Applies when the system is iterated enough times for emergence to manifest and the\nrules are local (interactions\
      \ are spatially or topologically nearby). Fails when:\n(1) rule application is too sparse to propagate; (2) rules are\
      \ globally coupled\n(everything affects everything \u2014 no local structure); (3) the parameter space is\nnot explored\
      \ systematically.\n"
    evidence_passages:
    - 'Flocking emerges from three local rules: alignment, cohesion, and separation'
    - The designer's role shifts from drawing the artifact to designing the rules
    - Simple programs can produce behavior of great complexity
    depth: cross-domain
    evidence: cited
    jargon:
    - emergence
    - generative system
    - cellular automata
    - local rules
    keywords:
    - generative art
    - emergence
    - complexity
    - rules
    application: Generative art, procedural content generation, simulation, data art
    elaboration: "The same principle underlies Conway's Game of Life, reaction-diffusion systems,\nand L-systems \u2014 all\
      \ generate complex forms from simple local rules."
    prerequisite_fbs: []
    contradicts_fbs:
    - 'TOP-DOWN-001: Top-Down Design Control'
    related_fbs:
    - 'PATCH-001: Procedural Patterning'
    procedural_skill: "When building a generative system: (1) define 3-7 simple local rules; (2) iterate\nthe system many\
      \ times; (3) map the parameter space; (4) curate outputs \u2014 not all\nemergent forms are interesting; (5) treat the\
      \ rule set as the primary artifact.\n"
    failure_mode: "If rules are too complex (many global conditions), emergence collapses into\nscripted behavior and the\
      \ output space shrinks to the designer's imagination \u2014\nthe generative advantage is lost.\n"
  rationale: 'Shiffman, Gross, and Wolfram converge on emergence from simple rules. Demonstrates

    Computational Art group, contradicts_fbs, depth, procedural_skill.

    '
- id: CONV-016
  domain: illustration
  discipline: color theory
  source_books:
  - "Interaction of Color \u2014 Josef Albers"
  - "Color and Light \u2014 James Gurney"
  - "The Elements of Color \u2014 Johannes Itten"
  cluster_segments:
  - source_book: "Interaction of Color \u2014 Josef Albers"
    text: "Colors are the most relative medium in art. A color's appearance changes completely\nbased on its neighbors. Restricting\
      \ the palette to a few colors forces the artist to\nunderstand these relationships deeply \u2014 with fewer colors,\
      \ each one must work harder\nand its interaction with others becomes the entire composition.\n"
  - source_book: "Color and Light \u2014 James Gurney"
    text: "Professional illustrators often restrict their palette deliberately. A limited\npalette creates harmony because\
      \ all colors share common pigment characteristics. The\nbeginner's urge to use every color available usually produces\
      \ mud \u2014 too many hues\ncompeting creates visual noise rather than richness.\n"
  - source_book: "The Elements of Color \u2014 Johannes Itten"
    text: "Color harmony is a matter of proportion and relationship, not the number of colors.\nItten's seven color contrasts\
      \ show that even two colors can produce powerful effects\nwhen their relationship is understood \u2014 complementary\
      \ contrast, simultaneous\ncontrast, and warm-cool contrast all emerge from pairings, not palettes.\n"
  is_convergent: true
  should_extract: true
  expected_fb:
    is_summary: false
    route: FB
    name: Limited Palette Discipline
    definition: 'Restricting the color palette to a small set of deliberate colors (typically 2-5)

      produces more harmonious and powerful visual work than using a wide palette,

      because color harmony is a function of relationships and proportion, not quantity.

      '
    mechanism: "With few colors, each color's appearance is determined by its interaction with the\nothers, forcing the artist\
      \ to understand simultaneous contrast and relative\nappearance. Wide palettes dilute this understanding \u2014 every\
      \ color is diluted by\ncompeting relationships, producing visual noise (mud).\n"
    consequence: 'Artists working within constraints produce more coherent work: the palette becomes

      a unifying element, and the limited vocabulary forces compositional clarity. This

      applies to illustration, UI design, branding, and data visualization.

      '
    boundary: 'Applies when the goal is harmony and coherence. Fails when: (1) the subject

      requires naturalistic color (photorealism) where wide gamut is necessary;

      (2) the palette is so limited it cannot represent required distinctions

      (colorblind-safe data viz needs distinguishable hues); (3) the artist lacks

      understanding of the colors chosen (limited palette without mastery = flat work).

      '
    evidence_passages:
    - Restricting the palette to a few colors forces the artist to understand these relationships deeply
    - The beginner's urge to use every color available usually produces mud
    - even two colors can produce powerful effects when their relationship is understood
    depth: domain
    evidence: cited
    jargon:
    - simultaneous contrast
    - palette
    - mud
    - color harmony
    keywords:
    - color
    - palette
    - illustration
    - harmony
    application: Illustration, branding, UI design, data visualization, photography
    elaboration: 'The principle generalizes beyond color: constraints in general (limited typefaces,

      limited materials, limited tools) often produce more creative work because they

      force deeper engagement with the material at hand.'
    prerequisite_fbs:
    - 'COLOR-REL-001: Color Relationship Basics'
    contradicts_fbs: []
    related_fbs:
    - 'COMP-CONTRAST-001: Complementary Contrast'
    procedural_skill: 'When starting a visual piece: (1) choose 2-5 colors from a single hue family or a

      complementary pair; (2) define proportions (dominant, secondary, accent); (3) test

      all pairings for simultaneous contrast; (4) adjust values (lightness) before

      saturating; (5) add one ''escape'' color only if the composition demands it.

      '
    failure_mode: "A palette chosen without understanding of value contrast (all colors same\nlightness) produces flat, unreadable\
      \ work even with few colors \u2014 the constraint\nmust be paired with value discipline.\n"
  rationale: 'Albers, Gurney, and Itten converge on limited-palette harmony. Demonstrates

    Illustration & Craft group, application, elaboration, procedural_skill.

    '
- id: NEG-001
  domain: finance & investment
  discipline: finance
  source_books:
