- id: NEG-005
  domain: systems & frameworks
  discipline: systems thinking
  source_books:
  - "Thinking in Systems \u2014 Donella Meadows"
  - "The Feynman Lectures on Physics \u2014 Richard Feynman"
  cluster_segments:
  - source_book: "Thinking in Systems \u2014 Donella Meadows"
    text: "Leverage points are places within a complex system where a small shift in one thing\ncan produce big changes\
      \ in everything. Leverage is not about force or power; it is\nabout finding the right point of intervention. The same\
      \ small change applied at a\nlow-leverage point does almost nothing, while applied at a high-leverage point it can\
      \ntransform the whole system.\n"
  - source_book: "The Feynman Lectures on Physics \u2014 Richard Feynman"
    text: "A lever gives mechanical advantage: a small force applied at a long distance from\nthe fulcrum can lift a heavy\
      \ weight at a short distance. The ratio of the distances\ntrades distance for force. Archimedes understood leverage\
      \ as pure geometry \u2014 the\nbalance point and the distances determine the outcome, nothing else.\n"
  is_convergent: false
  should_extract: false
  expected_fb:
    is_summary: false
    route: 'NULL'
    name: Leverage (jargon echo)
    definition: ''
    mechanism: ''
    boundary: ''
    consequence: ''
    evidence_passages: []
  rationale: "HARD NEGATIVE \u2014 JARGON ECHO. Both segments use the word 'leverage' but describe\nunrelated mechanisms:\
    \ Meadows means system intervention points (feedback structure),\nFeynman means mechanical force amplification (physics).\
    \ A naive matcher converges\non the shared term; the correct behavior is rejection because there is no shared\ncausal\
    \ mechanism. Same vocabulary, disjoint claims.\n"
- id: NEG-006
  domain: education
  discipline: learning science
  source_books:
  - "Make It Stick \u2014 Brown, Roediger & McDaniel"
  - "Moonwalking with Einstein \u2014 Joshua Foer"
  cluster_segments:
  - source_book: "Make It Stick \u2014 Brown, Roediger & McDaniel"
    text: "Repeated retrieval strengthens memory for facts and concepts. In experiments, students\nwho practiced retrieving\
      \ studied material at spaced intervals remembered\nsubstantially more on delayed tests than students who re-read the\
      \ material. Spacing\nand testing effects are among the most robust findings in cognitive psychology.\n"
  - source_book: "Moonwalking with Einstein \u2014 Joshua Foer"
    text: "Memory athletes memorize decks of cards and long strings of digits using spaced\nretrieval: reviewing the material\
      \ at expanding intervals commits it to long-term\nmemory. The technique works for facts, names, and lists \u2014 declarative\
      \ knowledge\nthat can be rehearsed and retrieved.\n"
  is_convergent: true
  should_extract: false
  expected_fb:
    is_summary: false
    route: 'NULL'
    name: Spaced Retrieval Universality (boundary violation)
    definition: ''
    mechanism: ''
    boundary: ''
    consequence: ''
    evidence_passages: []
  rationale: "HARD NEGATIVE \u2014 BOUNDARY VIOLATION. The two sources genuinely converge on a\nscoped claim: spaced retrieval\
    \ improves DECLARATIVE memory (facts, concepts,\nlists). A candidate FB claiming 'spaced repetition improves all learning'\
    \ over-generalizes:\nneither source studies procedural/motor skills or emotional learning. The correct\nextraction keeps\
    \ the boundary (declarative knowledge); the over-broad FB must be\nrejected. is_convergent: true but should_extract: false\
    \ because the presented\nprinciple exceeds the evidence.\n"
- id: CONV-017
  domain: education
  discipline: cognitive science
  source_books:
  - "Make It Stick \u2014 Peter Brown"
  - "Moonwalking with Einstein \u2014 Joshua Foer"
  - "Why We Sleep \u2014 Matthew Walker"
  cluster_segments:
  - source_book: "Make It Stick \u2014 Peter Brown"
    text: 'Spaced practice is far more effective than massed practice. Retrieving a memory

      at increasing intervals strengthens it because each retrieval is harder and

      requires deeper reconstruction. Cramming produces short-term gains that fade

      quickly, while spaced retrieval produces durable long-term learning.

      '
  - source_book: "Moonwalking with Einstein \u2014 Joshua Foer"
    text: "Memory athletes do not have exceptional brains \u2014 they use systematic techniques.\nThe spacing effect is the\
      \ single most powerful memory technique: reviewing\nmaterial at expanding intervals produces dramatically better retention\
      \ than\nequal-interval or massed review.\n"
  - source_book: "Why We Sleep \u2014 Matthew Walker"
    text: "Sleep consolidates memory. Memories are transferred from the hippocampus to the\nneocortex during sleep, making\
      \ them stable and integrated. This is why spaced\npractice with sleep between sessions works better than cramming \u2014\
      \ each sleep\nperiod consolidates what was practiced that day.\n"
  is_convergent: true
  should_extract: true
  expected_fb:
    is_summary: false
    route: FB
    name: Spaced Retrieval Practice
    definition: 'Retrieving information at expanding intervals produces dramatically more durable

      learning than massed practice (cramming). Each spaced retrieval forces deeper

      reconstruction of the memory, and intervening sleep consolidates it into

      long-term storage.

      '
    mechanism: 'Spaced retrieval works through the testing effect (retrieval strengthens memory

      more than re-study) amplified by the spacing effect (intervals allow partial

      forgetting that makes retrieval effortful) and sleep consolidation (hippocampal-

      neocortical transfer during sleep stabilizes memories).

      '
    consequence: 'Learners using spaced retrieval retain substantially more after weeks and months

      than learners who crammed the same total time. The effect is robust across

      subjects, ages, and materials.

      '
    boundary: "Applies to declarative knowledge and procedural skills. Fails when: (1) the\nmaterial must be recalled within\
      \ hours (urgent exam) \u2014 spacing still helps but\nmassed practice is faster for same-day recall; (2) the learner\
      \ does not actually\nretrieve (re-reading is not retrieval practice); (3) intervals are too long,\ncausing complete\
      \ forgetting before the next review.\n"
    evidence_passages:
    - Spaced practice is far more effective than massed practice
    - The spacing effect is the single most powerful memory technique
    - 'each sleep period consolidates what was practiced that day'
    depth: universal
    evidence: cited
    jargon:
    - testing effect
    - spacing effect
    - hippocampal-neocortical transfer
    keywords:
    - spaced repetition
    - memory
    - retrieval
    - learning
    application: Study systems, corporate training, language learning (Anki, SuperMemo)
    elaboration: 'The spacing effect is one of the most replicated findings in cognitive psychology

      (first demonstrated by Ebbinghaus in 1885) and is the theoretical basis of

      spaced repetition software (SRS).'
    prerequisite_fbs:
    - 'RETRIEVAL-BASE-001: Retrieval Practice Basics'
    contradicts_fbs:
    - 'CRAMMING-001: Massed Practice Efficacy'
    related_fbs:
    - 'SLEEP-CONSOL-001: Sleep Consolidation'
    procedural_skill: "Design a spaced repetition schedule: Day 0 (learn), Day 1 (first recall), Day 3,\nDay 7, Day 14, Day\
      \ 30. Each review must be active retrieval (cover the answer,\nrecall it, then check) \u2014 never passive re-reading.\
      \ Adjust intervals when recall\nfails (shorten) or succeeds easily (lengthen).\n"
    failure_mode: 'SRS systems fail when users treat reviews as re-reading sessions: without active

      retrieval, the spacing effect collapses and retention matches massed practice.

      '
  rationale: 'Brown, Foer, and Walker converge on spaced retrieval with distinct mechanisms

    (testing effect, spacing effect, sleep consolidation). Demonstrates education domain,

    procedural_skill, contradicts_fbs.

    '
- id: CONV-018
  domain: business operations
  discipline: decision making
  source_books:
  - "Certain to Win \u2014 Chet Richards"
  - "The Art of Action \u2014 Stephen Bungay"
  - "Blink \u2014 Malcolm Gladwell"
  cluster_segments:
  - source_book: "Certain to Win \u2014 Chet Richards"
    text: 'The OODA loop (Observe, Orient, Decide, Act) is John Boyd''s model of decision

      superiority. The key insight is that speed of iteration matters more than

      optimization of any single step. Whoever cycles through the loop faster than

      their opponent forces the opponent into a reactive posture.

      '
  - source_book: "The Art of Action \u2014 Stephen Bungay"
    text: "In military command, the key is not to make the perfect plan but to create\nshared intent (the commander's intent)\
      \ and then let subordinates act decisively\nwithin it. Over-control slows the decision cycle and hands initiative to\
      \ the\nenemy. The objective is tempo \u2014 operating faster than the opponent's decision\ncycle.\n"
  - source_book: "Blink \u2014 Malcolm Gladwell"
    text: "Rapid cognition \u2014 'thin-slicing' \u2014 can outperform extensive deliberation in\ncertain domains. Experts\
      \ who have compressed years of experience into\npattern-recognition can make better decisions in seconds than novices\
      \ make in\nhours. Speed is not the enemy of accuracy; it is often its ally.\n"
  is_convergent: true
  should_extract: true
  expected_fb:
    is_summary: false
    route: FB
    name: Decision Tempo Superiority
    definition: 'In competitive situations, the agent that cycles through its decision loop

      (observe-orient-decide-act) faster than the opponent gains decisive advantage,

      because the opponent is forced into a reactive posture and loses the initiative.

      Operational tempo beats perfect optimization.

      '
    mechanism: "Faster cycling means each of the opponent's moves is anticipated or answered\nbefore it can be completed \u2014\
      \ the slower agent is always one step behind and\ncannot set the terms of engagement. Shared intent allows subordinate\n\
      decision-making without centralized delay, preserving tempo at scale.\n"
    consequence: 'Organizations that compress their decision cycles (empower local actors, clarify

      intent, reduce approval layers) consistently outmaneuver slower organizations

      even with inferior resources.

      '
    boundary: 'Applies to adversarial or competitive environments with time pressure and

      uncertainty. Fails when: (1) the domain requires deep analysis where speed

      sacrifices correctness (nuclear safety, medical diagnosis); (2) the team lacks

      competence to act on shared intent; (3) decisions have irreversible

      consequences that favor deliberation.

      '
    evidence_passages:
    - Whoever cycles through the loop faster than their opponent forces the opponent into a reactive posture
    - "The objective is tempo \u2014 operating faster than the opponent's decision cycle"
    - Experts who have compressed years of experience into pattern-recognition can make better decisions in seconds
    depth: cross-domain
    evidence: cited
    jargon:
    - OODA loop
    - commander's intent
    - tempo
    - thin-slicing
    keywords:
    - decision making
    - tempo
    - OODA
    - competition
    application: Competitive strategy, product development, military command, sports, sales
    elaboration: 'Boyd''s OODA loop originated in air combat but has been applied broadly. The

      mechanism is information-cycle asymmetry: each agent''s observation cycle is

      coupled to the other''s actions, so the faster agent controls the tempo.

      '
    prerequisite_fbs:
    - 'INTENT-001: Commander''s Intent'
    contradicts_fbs:
    - 'PERF-PLAN-001: Perfect Plan Preparation'
    related_fbs:
    - 'DECISION-COMP-001: Decision Compression'
    - 'EMPOWER-001: Subordinate Empowerment'
    procedural_skill: 'To implement tempo superiority: (1) define the commander''s intent in one

      paragraph (what and why, not how); (2) push decision authority to the lowest

      competent level; (3) eliminate approval layers that add latency; (4) set a

      bounded decision cadence (e.g., weekly sprints); (5) measure decision-cycle time

      and reduce it iteratively.

      '
    failure_mode: "Tempo without competence is chaos: if actors lack the skill to act on shared\nintent, faster cycling produces\
      \ faster mistakes. Tempo superiority requires\ncompetence floors \u2014 it is a force multiplier, not a substitute for\
      \ ability.\n"
  rationale: 'Richards, Bungay, and Gladwell converge on decision tempo as competitive advantage.

    Demonstrates procedural_skill, failure_mode, contradicts_fbs. Expands Business group.

    '
- id: CONV-019
  domain: health & wellness
  discipline: behavioral economics
  source_books:
  - "Atomic Habits \u2014 James Clear"
  - "The Power of Habit \u2014 Charles Duhigg"
  - "Tiny Habits \u2014 BJ Fogg"
  cluster_segments:
  - source_book: "Atomic Habits \u2014 James Clear"
    text: 'Habit stacking is a strategy for pairing a new habit with an existing one: ''After

      I [CURRENT HABIT], I will [NEW HABIT].'' The existing habit serves as a stable

      anchor and trigger for the new behavior. This works because the current habit

      already has a well-worn neural pathway that the new habit can attach to.

      '
  - source_book: "The Power of Habit \u2014 Charles Duhigg"
    text: 'The habit loop consists of cue, routine, and reward. New habits are easier to

      form when they are attached to an existing cue. Duhigg calls this ''the golden

      rule of habit change'': you cannot extinguish a bad habit, but you can change the

      routine while keeping the cue and reward.

      '
  - source_book: "Tiny Habits \u2014 BJ Fogg"
    text: 'Fogg''s formula is: after a reliable existing behavior (anchor), do a tiny new

      behavior, then celebrate immediately. The tiny size lowers friction to near

      zero, and the celebration attaches positive emotion, which wires the habit

      faster. Anchoring is the most reliable way to install a new habit.

      '
  is_convergent: true
  should_extract: true
  expected_fb:
    is_summary: false
    route: FB
    name: Habit Anchoring
    definition: 'Attaching a new behavior to an existing, reliable habit (as its immediate

      successor) dramatically increases the probability the new habit forms. The

      existing habit provides the cue; the new habit rides the established neural

      pathway.

      '
    mechanism: "Existing habits have strong, automatic cue-response wiring. By placing the new\nbehavior immediately after\
      \ a reliable anchor, the new behavior inherits the\nanchor's cue strength. Celebration after the tiny behavior attaches\
      \ positive\nreward, completing the loop (cue-anchor \u2192 new routine \u2192 celebration).\n"
    consequence: 'Habit anchoring converts the hardest part of habit formation (remembering to

      do the behavior) from a conscious decision into an automatic sequence.

      Formation rates are substantially higher than standalone habit attempts.

      '
    boundary: 'Applies when the anchor is genuinely reliable (performed daily without

      exception) and the new habit is small enough to complete in seconds. Fails

      when: (1) the anchor is aspirational, not actual; (2) the new habit is large

      (enough friction to interrupt the chain); (3) the environment changes

      (vacation, travel) breaking the anchor''s reliability.

      '
    evidence_passages:
    - Habit stacking is a strategy for pairing a new habit with an existing one
    - New habits are easier to form when they are attached to an existing cue
    - Anchoring is the most reliable way to install a new habit
    depth: domain
    evidence: cited
    jargon:
    - habit stacking
    - anchor
    - cue-routine-reward
    - tiny habits
    keywords:
    - habits
    - behavior change
    - anchoring
    application: Personal productivity, fitness, health interventions, therapy
    elaboration: 'Clear, Duhigg, and Fogg are the three leading popular-science authors on habit

      formation; all converge on the cue-anchor mechanism with minor terminology

      variations (stacking, golden rule, anchoring).'
    prerequisite_fbs:
    - 'LOOP-BASE-002: Habit Loop Structure'
    contradicts_fbs:
    - 'MOTIVATION-001: Motivation-Driven Change'
    related_fbs:
    - 'TINY-FIRST-001: Tiny Habit Scaling'
    - 'CELEBRATION-001: Reward Attachment'
    procedural_skill: 'Install a habit: (1) choose a daily anchor (''After I brush my teeth''); (2) make

      the new behavior tiny (2 minutes or less); (3) state the plan explicitly

      (''After I ___, I will ___''); (4) celebrate immediately after; (5) log

      completion; (6) scale up only after 2 weeks of consistency.

      '
    failure_mode: "If the anchor habit is itself unreliable, the chain breaks and the new habit\nfails silently \u2014 the\
      \ user thinks they lack willpower when the actual failure is\nanchor selection.\n"
  rationale: 'Clear, Duhigg, and Fogg converge on anchoring new habits to existing cues.

    Demonstrates health & wellness domain, prerequisite_fbs, related_fbs,

    procedural_skill.

    '
- id: CONV-020
  domain: legal & public policy
  discipline: political economy
  source_books:
  - "Nudge \u2014 Richard Thaler & Cass Sunstein"
  - "Thinking, Fast and Slow \u2014 Daniel Kahneman"
  - "The Undoing Project \u2014 Michael Lewis"
  cluster_segments:
  - source_book: "Nudge \u2014 Richard Thaler & Cass Sunstein"
    text: "Defaults are incredibly powerful. When a choice is framed with a default, most\npeople do not change it \u2014\
      \ they accept the option they are given. In retirement\nsavings, automatic enrollment (opt-out) produces participation\
      \ rates above 90%,\nwhile voluntary enrollment (opt-in) produces rates below 50%. Changing the\ndefault changes behavior\
      \ without restricting choice.\n"
  - source_book: "Thinking, Fast and Slow \u2014 Daniel Kahneman"
    text: 'Status quo bias is a manifestation of loss aversion and inertia. People

      overweight the losses associated with changing from the current state. This is

      why defaults matter: the default defines the status quo, and changing it

      requires overcoming loss aversion.

      '
  - source_book: "The Undoing Project \u2014 Michael Lewis"
    text: "Kahneman and Tversky's prospect theory showed that people evaluate outcomes\nrelative to a reference point, not\
      \ absolutely. The default sets the reference\npoint \u2014 any change is perceived as a loss or gain relative to it.\
      \ This is the\npsychological foundation of why default options stick.\n"
  is_convergent: true
  should_extract: true
  expected_fb:
    is_summary: false
    route: FB
    name: Default Option Stickiness
    definition: "When a choice architecture includes a default option, the majority of people\nwill accept the default rather\
      \ than actively choose an alternative. Changing\nthe default reliably changes aggregate behavior without prohibiting\
      \ any option\n\u2014 a form of libertarian paternalism.\n"
    mechanism: 'Defaults leverage status quo bias and loss aversion: the default becomes the

      reference point, and any deviation is evaluated as a potential loss. Combined

      with inertia and the effort cost of opting out, this produces high default

      acceptance rates.

      '
    consequence: 'Policies and products that set defaults deliberately (automatic enrollment,

      pre-checked options, standard configurations) achieve dramatically higher

      adoption than equivalent opt-in designs, at zero cost to choice freedom.

      '
    boundary: "Applies when the choice is one people are reluctant to deliberate on (complex,\nlow-stakes, or emotionally\
      \ neutral). Fails when: (1) the choice is highly\nconsequential (medical decisions) \u2014 people override defaults;\
      \ (2) the default\nis obviously harmful \u2014 backlash and opt-out increase; (3) the population has\nstrong pre-existing\
      \ preferences.\n"
    evidence_passages:
    - automatic enrollment (opt-out) produces participation rates above 90%
    - Status quo bias is a manifestation of loss aversion and inertia
    - people evaluate outcomes relative to a reference point, not absolutely
    depth: universal
    evidence: cited
    jargon:
    - choice architecture
    - default
    - status quo bias
    - nudge
    keywords:
    - default
    - nudge
    - behavioral economics
    - choice architecture
    application: Retirement policy, organ donation, insurance, software settings, consent forms
    elaboration: 'The default effect is one of the most replicated findings in behavioral

      economics, with field evidence from retirement savings (401k), organ donation

      (opt-out countries), and privacy settings.'
    prerequisite_fbs:
    - 'LOSS-AV-001: Loss Aversion Basics'
    contradicts_fbs:
    - 'RATIONAL-ACTOR-001: Rational Actor Model'
    related_fbs:
    - 'FRAMING-001: Choice Framing Effects'
    procedural_skill: 'When designing any choice architecture: (1) identify the desired social or

      individual outcome; (2) set the default to the option that produces it; (3)

      keep the alternative one step away (not hidden); (4) disclose the default and

      its rationale; (5) measure opt-out rate; (6) revisit if opt-out exceeds a

      threshold indicating coercion.

      '
    failure_mode: 'Setting defaults for consequential personal choices (medical, financial)

      without transparency backfires: perceived manipulation triggers reactance and

      erodes trust in the institution, causing long-term opt-out and reputation

      damage.

      '
  rationale: 'Thaler/Sunstein, Kahneman, and Lewis converge on default stickiness via status quo

    bias and reference points. Demonstrates legal & public policy domain, contradicts_fbs.

    '
- id: CONV-021
  domain: systems & frameworks
  discipline: systems thinking
  source_books:
  - "Thinking in Systems \u2014 Donella Meadows"
  - "Freakonomics \u2014 Steven Levitt & Stephen Dubner"
  - "The Logic of Life \u2014 Tim Harford"
  cluster_segments:
  - source_book: "Thinking in Systems \u2014 Donella Meadows"
    text: "Every action in a system has second-order effects \u2014 effects that were not\nintended and often not predicted.\
      \ Policy resistance occurs when the system\ncompensates for an intervention, producing the opposite of the intended\
      \ effect.\nThe cure for policy resistance is to find the source of the resistance and\nchange the rules that create\
      \ it.\n"
  - source_book: "Freakonomics \u2014 Steven Levitt & Stephen Dubner"
    text: 'Incentives are the cornerstone of modern life. When the incentive structure is

      misaligned, behavior follows the incentive, not the stated goal. The authors

      document how well-intentioned policies (e.g., Chicago school reform) created

      unintended behaviors (teaching to the test, cheating) when the incentive

      metric was gameable.

      '
  - source_book: "The Logic of Life \u2014 Tim Harford"
    text: 'The law of unintended consequences: interventions that ignore the rational

      responses of the people they target will be evaded or exploited. Rational

      economic agents respond to incentives in ways that the policy designer often

      fails to anticipate.

      '
  is_convergent: true
  should_extract: true
  expected_fb:
    is_summary: false
    route: FB
    name: Incentive-Driven Unintended Consequences
    definition: 'Any intervention that changes the incentive structure of a system will produce

      responses that the designer did not intend, because the agents in the system

      optimize against the new incentives rather than the stated goal. This is the

      fundamental source of policy resistance and gaming behavior.

      '
    mechanism: 'Agents in any system respond to incentives, not intentions. When an

      intervention alters the payoff structure, agents re-optimize their behavior;

      this re-optimization often exploits gaps in the intervention''s design,

      producing behaviors that counteract the stated goal (Goodhart''s law: when a

      measure becomes a target, it ceases to be a good measure).

      '
    consequence: 'Policies and incentives must be designed with the expectation of gaming and

      adaptation. The metric used to measure success will be gamed if it is visible;

      the intervention will be evaded if it is costly; second-order effects will

      emerge from the interaction of rational agents.

      '
    boundary: 'Applies when the intervention changes observable incentives AND agents have

      freedom to respond. Fails when: (1) agents lack the information to perceive

      incentive changes; (2) the system is closed with no adaptive freedom; (3) the

      intervention is invisible to agents (they cannot react to what they cannot see).

      '
    evidence_passages:
    - Every action in a system has second-order effects
    - When the incentive structure is misaligned, behavior follows the incentive, not the stated goal
    - Interventions that ignore the rational responses of the people they target will be evaded or exploited
    depth: universal
    evidence: cited
    jargon:
    - second-order effects
    - policy resistance
    - Goodhart's law
    - incentive gaming
    keywords:
    - incentives
    - unintended consequences
    - systems
    - policy
    application: Public policy, management, product design, economics, regulation
    elaboration: 'This is the systems-theoretic restatement of Goodhart''s law and the cobra

      effect (the classic example of a bounty program that backfired).'
    prerequisite_fbs:
    - 'INCENT-BASE-001: Incentive Structure Analysis'
    contradicts_fbs:
    - 'STATIC-POLICY-001: Static Policy Sufficiency'
    related_fbs:
    - 'GOODHART-001: Metric Gaming'
    - 'POLICY-RESIST-001: Policy Resistance'
    procedural_skill: 'Before implementing any policy or metric: (1) model the incentive change;

      (2) ask who can game the new metric and how; (3) predict second-order effects

      (what will the system do to compensate?); (4) design countermeasures;

      (5) pilot on a small scale and watch for gaming; (6) review the metric itself

      periodically.

      '
    failure_mode: 'Using a metric as a target without monitoring gaming leads to Goodhart collapse:

      the metric rises while the underlying goal deteriorates (e.g., call-center

      handle-time targets reducing service quality).

      '
  rationale: 'Meadows, Levitt/Dubner, and Harford converge on incentive-driven unintended

    consequences. Demonstrates contradiction, related_fbs, procedural_skill.

    '
